 "lean": 11.0, "environment": 8.2, "incumbency": 2.0, "money": 1.13}, "rating_margin": 20.0, "sigma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 7.22, "baseline_margin": 21.39, "analyst_adjustment": -0.89, "key_factors": ["D+11 district with incumbent Pingree seeking re-election", "Late University of New Hampshire likely-voter poll shows Pingree ahead 57-35", "Pingree holds a roughly 20-to-1 cash-on-hand advantage over Russell", "Prediction markets price a Democratic win near 96 percent"], "rationale": "An eleven-point Democratic district, a double-digit Pingree lead in the one late poll, a dominant fundraising edge, and markets near 96 percent all point the same direction, and no outside money or ratings signal vulnerability. This lands as Safe Democratic.", "watch": "A strong Russell debate performance or a late wave of outside spending against Pingree is the main thing that could narrow the margin, though it would take far more to threaten the seat.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["No expert rater lists this race; treated as safe for the party holding the seat.", "Incumbent Chellie Pingree (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Chellie Pingree, the Democratic incumbent, seeks another term in Maine's 1st District. Republican Ronald Russell won his party's nomination in June, setting up a rematch with Pingree. The district's partisan lean favors Democrats by roughly eleven points, giving the incumbent a strong structural edge heading into the fall.\n\nA late-September University of New Hampshire survey of likely voters put Pingree ahead 57 to 35, and prediction markets price her chances at about 96 percent. She also holds a wide financial edge, with over $420,000 on hand compared with roughly $20,000 for Russell, and no outside groups have targeted the race. The campaign has been low-key, with the two meeting in just one debate days before the election. Barring an unexpected development, this race appears squarely in the safe Democratic category.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_